<section id="A1" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix A </span>Appendix</h2>

<section id="A1.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.1 </span>Details of Benchmarks</h3>

<section id="A1.SS1.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph"><span id="A1.SS1.SSS0.Px1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">The Berkeley Function-Calling Leaderboard (BFCL)</span>.</h5>

<div id="A1.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Patil et al. (2025)</a></cite> introduce a comprehensive benchmark BFCL for evaluating function-calling capabilities of LLMs, comprising two primary evaluation categories: Non-Live and Live.
Each category further includes four distinct task types: single, multiple, parallel, and parallel multiple function invocations. The total number of samples in the used test set is 2,501.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="A1.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="A1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Single</span>: LLMs are provided with a single function description and must generate exactly one corresponding call.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Multiple</span>: LLMs are provided with a set of candidate functions and must select and invoke the most appropriate one.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Parallel</span>: LLMs need to generate multiple function calls simultaneously for a single query, determining the appropriate number of invocations.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A1.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A1.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.I1.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Parallel Multiple</span>: Combining the challenges of <span id="A1.I1.i4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Parallel</span> and <span id="A1.I1.i4.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Multiple</span>, LLMs need to select from several candidates to invoke zero or more functions as required.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS1.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph"><span id="A1.SS1.SSS0.Px2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">ACEBench</span>.</h5>

<div id="A1.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">ACEBench <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al. (2025a)</a></cite> is a diverse tool-calling benchmark that offers fine-grained coverage across a broad range of domains.
Specifically, it encompasses 8 major domains and 68 sub-domains, including but not limited to technology, finance, entertainment, society, health, culture, and environment.
The benchmark is organized into three primary categories: Normal, Special, and Agent.
In this work, we focus exclusively on the <span id="A1.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Normal</span> category, which contains 828 test samples.
The Normal category is further subdivided into the following five tasks: <span id="A1.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Single-Turn</span>, <span id="A1.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Multi-Turn</span>, <span id="A1.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Similar APIs</span>, <span id="A1.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Profile</span>, and <span id="A1.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Atom</span>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.2 </span>Training and Inference Details</h3>

<div id="A1.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this work, we use the xlam-function-calling-60k dataset <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al. (2025a)</a></cite> as raw training corpus, and construct a CoT training set with 46,897 samples. Table <a href="#A1.T4" title="Table 4 ‣ A.2 Training and Inference Details ‣ Appendix A Appendix ‣ Try, Check and Retry: A Divide-and-Conquer Framework for Boosting Long-context Tool-Calling Performance of LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> shows the detailed training hyperparameters. Specifically, we set the batch size to 16 and the peak learning rate to 2e-5. All models are trained with LoRA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hu et al. (2021)</a></cite> for 2 epochs on two NVIDIA A800 80GB GPUs, requiring approximately 10 hours for finetuning 7B models using our Tool-DC (TB). For inference, we use the VLLM<span id="footnote3" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">3</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">3</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">3</span>
              
              
              
            https://github.com/vllm-project/vllm</span></span></span> engine for accelerating the model generation, and set the temperature to 0, the maximum length of output tokens to 512.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A1.T4" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 4: </span>Training hyperparameters for all models.</figcaption>
<div id="A1.T4.2" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_center ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:207.0pt;height:106.5pt;vertical-align:-49.6pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(32.4pt,-16.7pt) scale(1.45499238402449,1.45499238402449) ;">
<table id="A1.T4.2.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_align_middle">
<tr id="A1.T4.2.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="A1.T4.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_tt"><span id="A1.T4.2.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Hyperparameter</span></td>
<td id="A1.T4.2.1.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt"><span id="A1.T4.2.1.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Value</span></td></tr>
<tr id="A1.T4.2.1.2" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="A1.T4.2.1.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_t">Batch Size</td>
<td id="A1.T4.2.1.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">16</td></tr>
<tr id="A1.T4.2.1.3" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="A1.T4.2.1.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left">Learning Rate</td>
<td id="A1.T4.2.1.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">2e-5</td></tr>
<tr id="A1.T4.2.1.4" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="A1.T4.2.1.4.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left">LR Scheduler</td>
<td id="A1.T4.2.1.4.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">cosine</td></tr>
<tr id="A1.T4.2.1.5" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="A1.T4.2.1.5.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left">Warmup Ratio</td>
<td id="A1.T4.2.1.5.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">0.1</td></tr>
<tr id="A1.T4.2.1.6" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="A1.T4.2.1.6.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_bb">Epochs</td>
<td id="A1.T4.2.1.6.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb">2</td></tr>
</table>
</span></div>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.3 </span>Details of Compared Models</h3>

<div id="A1.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In our training-based experiments, we compare our tuned models with both general-purpose
commercial APIs (OpenAI o3<span id="footnote4" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">4</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">4</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">4</span>
              
              
              
            https://openai.com/index/introducing-o3-and-o4-mini</span></span></span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">OpenAI (2025)</a></cite>, DeepSeek-V3.2-Exp<span id="footnote5" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">5</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">5</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">5</span>
              
              
              
            https://api-docs.deepseek.com/news/news250929</span></span></span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al. (2025)</a></cite>, Claude-Haiku-4.5<span id="footnote6" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">6</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">6</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">6</span>
              
              
              
            https://www.anthropic.com/news/claude-haiku-4-5</span></span></span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">Anthropic (2025)</a></cite>, and Gemini-3-Pro-Preview<span id="footnote7" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">7</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">7</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">7</span>
              
              
              
            https://deepmind.google/models/gemini/pro/</span></span></span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">Pichai et al. (2025)</a></cite>)
and open-source large-scale models (Qwen3-235B-A22B-Inst <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al. (2025a)</a></cite> and Llama-3.3-70B-Inst <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dubey et al. (2024)</a></cite>), as well as some specialized tool-calling models, including ToolACE-DEV-8B <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Huang et al. (2025)</a></cite>, ToolACE-MT-8B <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zeng et al. (2025)</a></cite>, xLAM-2 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al. (2025a)</a></cite>, and Hammer2.1(1.5B/3B/7B) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lin et al. (2025)</a></cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.4 </span>Sensitivity Analysis of Group Count <math id="A1.SS4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math></h3>

<div id="A1.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We extend the sensitivity analysis of group count <math id="A1.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> to Qwen2.5-1.5B, 3B, and 7B-Instruct. Results in Figure <a href="#A1.F8" title="Figure 8 ‣ A.4 Sensitivity Analysis of Group Count 𝐾 ‣ Appendix A Appendix ‣ Try, Check and Retry: A Divide-and-Conquer Framework for Boosting Long-context Tool-Calling Performance of LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a> consistently show that while baselines like <span id="A1.SS4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Top-K</span> suffer significant drops at low <math id="A1.SS4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> (<math id="A1.SS4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="&lt;3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">&lt;3</annotation></semantics></math>) due to over-filtering, our method remains robust even at <math id="A1.SS4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K=1</annotation></semantics></math>. Performance stabilizes at <math id="A1.SS4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K\in[4,6]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K\in[4,6]</annotation></semantics></math>, confirming that a moderate partition size effectively balances noise reduction and context completeness across varying model scales.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A1.F8" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2603.11495v1/figure8.svg" id="A1.F8.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:405/466;" width="405" height="466"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 8: </span>
Sensitivity analysis of group count <math id="A1.F8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> across Qwen2.5 series. All models are evaluated on the Extended Setting of BFCL.
</figcaption>
</figure>
